Finding: {"title":"db-health.ts migration-count check silently swallows errors and Sentry-init failures in production","severity":"LOW","area":"Observability / health checks","files":["src/lib/db-health.ts"],"detail":"assertMigrationsUpToDate() (src/lib/db-health.ts:13) catches all errors in production and only logs to console + best-effort Sentry, itself wrapped in a swallowed try/catch (lines 25-29 and 34-38). If the query fails in production (drizzle schema missing, permission denied, connectivity issue), this returns silently with no distinction from 'check ran and passed'.","impact":"Low — health-check nicety, not a data-integrity path. But the double-swallow makes it possible for a check meant to give 'ops visibility' to fail completely invisibly if Sentry also throws at the same time the DB query fails.","recommendation":"Distinguish 'check ran and passed', 'check ran and found mismatch', and 'check could not run' in the log line/metric so silent failure-to-check doesn't look identical to a clean pass.","confidence":"medium"}
